Guard printValueAsJSON's recursion with the max-call-depth counter (2.31 only).

nix::printValueAsJSON (src/libexpr/value-to-json.cc) recurses through attrs
and list elements. It stops at `__toString` and at `outPath`, which is why a
derivation -- cyclic though it is -- converts to its store path instead of
recursing forever. A cycle with neither, e.g. `let x = { y = x; }; in x`, has
no such stopping rule, so on 2.31 the recursion is bounded only by the C++
stack: it overflows and the process takes SIGSEGV. That is not an error a
caller can catch, it kills the whole interpreter, and nanopynix reaches this
function from `Value.to_python()` / `Value.to_json()` -- so an ordinary
library call on an ordinary (if silly) Nix value takes the host process down.

Upstream fixed this by routing the recursion through EvalState's existing
call-depth counter, which raises nix::EvalBaseError ("stack overflow;
max-call-depth exceeded") instead. Verified by diffing the 2.31 and 2.34
sources: 2.34's copy of this function opens with `state.addCallDepth(pos)`
and 2.31's does not, and the two files are otherwise identical here. The
release in between is gone from nixpkgs (`nix_2_32`/`nix_2_33` both throw
"has been removed"), so 2.31 is the only version we build that still needs
this, and the fix is unnecessary -- not merely redundant -- from 2.34 on.

The call is a straight backport, not a reimplementation: EvalState::addCallDepth
already exists in 2.31 (src/libexpr/include/nix/expr/eval-inline.hh) with the
same signature and the same "throw once callDepth passes settings.maxCallDepth"
body that 2.34 has, and value-to-json.cc already includes eval-inline.hh. The
returned CallDepth is an RAII counter, so `_level` is deliberately unused --
it decrements on scope exit.

diff -ruN a/src/libexpr/value-to-json.cc b/src/libexpr/value-to-json.cc
--- a/src/libexpr/value-to-json.cc
+++ b/src/libexpr/value-to-json.cc
@@ -16,6 +16,8 @@
 {
     checkInterrupt();
 
+    auto _level = state.addCallDepth(pos);
+
     if (strict)
         state.forceValue(v, pos);
 
